---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    number: '01',
    label: 'Direct extension · 2016',
    title: 'Beyond the Twelve-Factor App',
    body: 'Kevin Hoffman offered the most direct published extension of the original methodology. He kept the core model and named three omissions: API-first design, telemetry, and authentication and authorization. Those map directly to Factors XI, XIII, and XII in this guide.',
    points: [
      'API-first becomes Contract-First Interfaces so the principle also covers events, queues, CLIs, and data contracts.',
      'Telemetry becomes Observability & SLOs so signals are connected to user-visible reliability decisions.',
      'Authentication and authorization becomes Identity & Least Privilege, covering people, workloads, devices, and short-lived credentials.',
    ],
    href: 'https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/beyond-the-twelve-factor/9781492042631/',
    source: 'O’Reilly Media',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    label: 'Reliability · SRE',
    title: 'Measure behavior, engineer for failure',
    body: 'Google’s Site Reliability Engineering books made several post-2011 obligations concrete: define service-level indicators and objectives, use error budgets to balance reliability and change, automate repeatable operations, canary releases, and learn through incident response and blameless postmortems.',
    points: [
      'Factors XIII, XV, XVIII, and XX turn those practices into application-level design constraints.',
      'A dashboard is not a reliability objective. A release pipeline is not safe merely because it is automated.',
      'The system must expose the signals and control points that operations need before launch, not after the first outage.',
    ],
    href: 'https://sre.google/sre-book/table-of-contents/',
    source: 'Google SRE',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    label: 'Cloud native · CNCF',
    title: 'Observable, portable, interoperable, available',
    body: 'The CNCF reference architecture describes cloud-native systems in terms that go beyond packaging: workloads should be observable, portable, interoperable, and available. OpenTelemetry supplies a vendor-neutral signal model; OpenGitOps defines declarative, versioned, automatically reconciled desired state.',
    points: [
      'Factor XI focuses on semantic interoperability rather than a particular port or protocol.',
      'Factor XIII uses traces, metrics, logs, and profiles as correlated evidence—not isolated tooling silos.',
      'Factor XVII treats infrastructure and policy as reviewable desired state and continuously detects drift.',
    ],
    href: 'https://architecture.cncf.io/',
    source: 'Cloud Native Computing Foundation',
  },
  {
    number: '04',
    label: 'Security · NIST / OpenSSF',
    title: 'Security is a lifecycle property',
    body: 'NIST’s Secure Software Development Framework says secure practices must be integrated into each software-development lifecycle. Its Zero Trust architecture removes implicit network trust. SLSA adds verifiable build provenance and increasing supply-chain assurance.',
    points: [
      'Factor XII requires explicit identity and authorization at every protected resource boundary.',
      'Factor XIV requires locked dependencies, isolated builds, SBOMs, provenance, signatures, and admission verification.',
      'Security claims must be backed by machine-verifiable evidence and exercised incident response.',
    ],
    href: 'https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final',
    source: 'NIST SSDF · SLSA',
  },
  {
    number: '05',
    label: 'Data · NIST Privacy',
    title: 'Design the complete data lifecycle',
    body: 'The NIST Privacy Framework treats processing as a complete lifecycle: collection, retention, transformation, use, disclosure, sharing, transmission, and disposal. A system that can store data but cannot explain, export, minimize, or delete it is operationally incomplete.',
    points: [
      'Factor XVI makes classification, minimization, retention, deletion, and recovery behavior part of the design.',
      'Privacy work starts during planning and is verified through build, deploy, operation, and decommissioning.',
      'Backups, analytics copies, logs, derived features, and model inputs belong to the same lifecycle map.',
    ],
    href: 'https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework/privacy-framework',
    source: 'NIST Privacy Framework',
  },
  {
    number: '06',
    label: 'Evolution · SemVer / Kubernetes',
    title: 'Compatibility is a production feature',
    body: 'Semantic Versioning begins with a declared public API and communicates incompatible change explicitly. Kubernetes goes further with formal API deprecation and data-conversion guarantees. Both reflect the same lesson: distributed consumers do not upgrade atomically.',
    points: [
      'Factor XIX requires additive-first changes, explicit versioning, deprecation telemetry, migration windows, and removal criteria.',
      'Database schemas, event payloads, configuration, and operational procedures are contracts too.',
      'Progressive delivery controls exposure; compatibility controls coexistence. They are related but not interchangeable.',
    ],
    href: 'https://semver.org/',
    source: 'Semantic Versioning · Kubernetes API',
  },
  {
    number: '07',
    label: 'Economics · FinOps',
    title: 'Usage is an engineering decision',
    body: 'The FinOps Foundation puts accountability for technology usage at the edge: engineers should own cost from architecture through ongoing operation, with timely and accurate data. That turns spend from a monthly surprise into a feedback signal.',
    points: [
      'Factor XXI assigns cost to workloads, tenants, features, and business outcomes.',
      'Budgets and anomaly detection are operational controls; unit economics makes trade-offs visible.',
      'The aim is not indiscriminate cost cutting. It is maximizing delivered value under explicit constraints.',
    ],
    href: 'https://www.finops.org/framework/principles/',
    source: 'FinOps Foundation',
  },
  {
    number: '08',
    label: 'Sustainability · Green Software',
    title: 'Efficiency has a physical footprint',
    body: 'The Green Software Foundation identifies energy efficiency, carbon awareness, and hardware efficiency as the direct levers software teams can use to reduce emissions. AWS likewise added sustainability as a Well-Architected pillar alongside reliability, security, and cost.',
    points: [
      'Factor XXII measures useful work per unit of energy, carbon, water, and hardware—not just aggregate cloud spend.',
      'Right-sizing, demand shaping, data minimization, and carbon-aware scheduling frequently improve cost and reliability too.',
      'Teams document trade-offs so sustainability work does not quietly violate latency, privacy, or availability needs.',
    ],
    href: 'https://learn.greensoftware.foundation/introduction/',
    source: 'Green Software Foundation',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Research Trail — The Twenty-Two-Factor App"
  description="The sources, critiques, and design decisions behind the Twenty-Two-Factor App methodology."
>
  <section class="content-page">
    <header class="content-page-header">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">Annotated research trail</p>
        <h1>Evidence before<br />enumeration.</h1>
      </div>
      <p>
        Twenty-two is not a magic number. It is an editorial compression of the
        strongest recurring gaps in post-2011 cloud-native practice. This page makes
        the compression—and the judgment behind it—inspectable.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="research-list">
      {sources.map((item) => (
        <article class="research-entry">
          <div class="research-entry-meta">
            <span>{item.number}</span>
            {item.label}
          </div>
          <div>
            <h2>{item.title}</h2>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
            <ul>
              {item.points.map((point) => <li>{point}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <a href={item.href} rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the primary source: {item.source} ↗</a>
          </div>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
